title The claim a sweep cannot follow
prompt the standing fleet baton -- take the next agent-doable lap in the mantra and tally lane
rota lap 4189, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; its cardinal seat, the clock and the mark, holds that a stamp says WHEN and a name says WHAT, and that a mark promises only what the work can keep. Read through earth's sense -- take in the concrete fact at the door before any argument about it -- that reading pointed straight at this lap: a ledger row whose stamp never moved while its number moved four times, and a Door page whose claim about the tree was concrete, checkable, and false.
think THE MECHANISM, PLAINLY. The cold roster refused with run_verdict=guard_red: 172 guards, 167 green, 2 red, 3 gated. The two reds were one root -- standing_equipment reds when another guard does, by its own red_self line -- so the single real red was stash_record, which asserts unlanded=0 and read four session logs standing in fleet_round_open.sh stashes with nothing carrying them. Twelve tracked paths came out of stash@{1} and stash@{0} with `git checkout <stash> -- <path>`, which reads the TREE object so the mode rides along, where `git show <stash>:<path> >` would have landed every one at 100644 (the exec-bit law's own clause).
think Before any of that the lap had to clear its own door. The first cold pass refused with run_lock=in_flight: a --hot runner from a dead lap held construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d, its process group leader gone, its output reaching nobody. The runner names its own repair -- kill -TERM releases the lock through its EXIT trap where SIGKILL bypasses it -- and one bounded TERM by pid, never `pkill -f`, is what %569 booked three hours earlier for reaching across a pier eight ships share.
think THE LANE WORK IS A FENCE THAT WAS MISSING, and it was written by a prior lap of this seat that never got to ship. mantra/src/diff.rye builds an LCS table of (m + 1) * (n + 1) cells of u32 in one allocator.alloc call from two lengths a caller chooses, and it carried no named max, no named error, and no edge check -- so its two `usize -> u32` seam casts ran with the bound that makes them safe unstated, which is the one thing TAME's width law asks of a seam cast. Two documents of 100,000 lines each would have asked for 40 GB in one call, and the ask would have been made rather than refused.
think The repair imports rather than restates: max_diff_lines IS weave.max_weave_lines, because a diff's old side comes from Weave.current and two constants naming one quantity are two constants that will one day disagree. max_diff_table_bytes is stated in BYTES, since bytes are what the machine has, and max_diff_table_cells is derived from it by @sizeOf(u32). check_bounds is a FUNCTION rather than two inline ifs, so a witness can press the boundary at the ceiling and one cell past without building a million-line document to do it.
think Proven on metal this lap: mantra_diff GREEN, ten control phases against a clean pen at 0; width_check_th3 GREEN; dated_spelling, reds_citation and ascii_document GREEN, the three fixtures the second stash carried.
obs THE LEDGER'S ALLOCATOR COLLIDED WITH ITSELF INSIDE ONE CHECKOUT, and this is the row worth keeping. Both parked laps booked %572. Neither was careless and neither could have seen the other: `reds_spine_derive_scan.sh --next` answers from the anointed spine plus this tree's COMMITTED rows, and each row was sitting in a stash rather than a commit, so the second lap read a spine the first lap's row was not in. A peer then published a third, unrelated row at %572 upstream. One number, three rows, two trees.
obs What that adds to the law: the derived-spine page reasons throughout about "three stars writing into this tree from three hosts", and every one of its six recorded firings is a pair of trees. The class is wider than its own evidence. A number allocated by reading a tree is allocated per COMMITTED tree, and the round-open's stash makes an uncommitted row ordinary rather than exotic -- so one checkout with two parked laps reproduces the fault exactly, with no second host involved at all.
obs And rule 4 paid for itself a fourth time on one row. The fence row booked %561, renumbered to %570, then %572, and lands as %574 -- while its stamp 20260907.111135 never moved and every living citation already spelled the stamp. Four renumberings touched the shelf filename, its two internal headings, the recital line, and nothing else. Cite by stamp until the row is shared is not a nicety; it is what confined four repairs to five lines.
obs I DID NOT TEACH --next TO READ THE STASHES. It would work, and it would bind the ledger's allocator to one launcher's private mechanism -- a stash is not a record, which is the whole thing stash_record exists to say. The cheaper answer is the one already seated: land the lap.
obs THE LANE'S OTHER FINDING IS A CLAIM NO SWEEP COULD FOLLOW, and I measured it rather than repairing it, because foundations are not this seat's room. foundations/20260825-211056_what-mantra-is.md is Door-tier, written for a complete beginner, and it says Mantra's weave is "a second promise, named at its christening and still unbuilt". mantra/src/weave.rye holds empty, apply, current, merge and annotate; six guards on standing-equipment.kyri read it every lap; mantra_weave_merge and mantra_weave_annotate both ran GREEN on metal this lap, the merge proving one answer in all six orders of three states and the annotation proving five readings with ten breaks caught.
obs The same page carries a second error pointing the other way, and this one was never true. Under "The chemical formula" it defines THE WEAVE as a bulk read of bindings -- weave(name_1 ... name_n) -> bytes_1 ... bytes_n -- and no such operation exists anywhere in mantra/. A grep of mantra/*.rye for the word finds nothing outside src/, and mantra/README.md names weave.rye alone. So one Door page uses one word for two structures, neither of them the one the code holds, and then tells the reader the real one is unbuilt.
obs WHY %441'S REPAIR COULD NOT REACH IT. That row caught this exact claim in the design essay and swept the essay, the roster, the card, the ledger and the seat prompt -- fourteen files, and not the foundation. The sweep was aimed at the page that MADE the claim and at the instrument that would catch it next time. Nobody asked which other pages had copied the claim, because that question has no tool: a path breaks loudly and has a resolver, a repointer and three commit-time link walls, while a stale claim resolves perfectly forever. It is prose, and prose never 404s.
obs THE CLASS IS AT LEAST TWO, measured by the same grep. foundations/20260618-184912_growing-a-language.md tells a first-day reader "Rishi does not exist yet" -- beside 2,401 tracked .rish scripts, a 3,491-line rishi/src/main.rye, a rishi/README.md reading "Status: Checkable -- shell, small and growing", and tools/p/parity.rish standing. That page named its own falsifier in its own closing paragraph: "When parity.rish turns green ... Rishi will have been born the right way." The falsifier fired and the page never heard. Its sentence was TRUE when written and load-bearing to its argument, which is why the honest repair there is an appended erratum rather than a rewrite -- the value of a plan is that it was written before.
obs NAMED, NOT BUILT, AND WHY. The instrument this suggests is a scan reading living prose for a negative-existence phrase near a module room this tree owns, and asking whether that room holds tracked authored source. It would have to be a ratchet reported and never gated: TAME_GUIDANCE's own honest "a supplement that does not exist yet is not overdue" sits one window from the word Glow and would read as a hit, and a meter that reds on honest Door prose is a meter somebody turns off. That is a lap with its own shape, and a recovery is not the place to open one -- the same reasoning the parked lap used for leaving width_check_th3 unrostered, which I also left standing.
obs THE CARD IS AT ITS CEILING AND IT TAXED THIS LAP EXACTLY AS THE PINS RULE SAYS IT WOULD. construction/ITINERARY.md opened 16 bytes under 40,960 with eight ships' accounts on one live front. My first account ran 4,106 bytes against the elder's 1,336, and reaching the bound took eleven passes of condensing my own prose -- no cairn owed, since every byte cut was written this lap. It lands at exactly 40,960 with zero headroom, which is a fact about the shared card rather than about this account: the next ship to write a fuller account than the one it folds has nowhere to put it.
obs THE ROW ABOUT RENUMBERING RENUMBERED AGAIN WHILE IT WAS BEING SENT, which is the finding proving itself. The fence row booked %561, was moved to %570, to %572, landed here as %574 -- and the rebase onto xy/main answered double_booked %574, because a peer had published a row at that number bound to stamp 20260907.144904 during this lap. Mine is unshared and theirs is published, so rule 3 decides without a judgment call: my three rows shift up one, to %575 fence, %576 spelling, %577 collision, keeping number order and stamp order the same order. Five renumberings on one row, and its stamp 20260907.111135 has not moved once.
obs A PEER'S RED RODE IN WITH THE REBASE, and it is one guard blind to what the guard beside it was taught to see. reds_ledger_monotone refuses verdict=number_double_bound because %512 (20260906.202423) -- ONE row, ONE stamp -- stands folded onto two shelves upstream, REDS-a-pen-named-by-its-purpose-rows-512.md and REDS-a-pen-one-path-wide-for-eight-ships-rows-512.md, both ancestors of xy/main and neither touched by this commit. reds_spine_derive_scan.sh reads (number, stamp) BINDINGS and answers published_doubles=1 naming only %530; it cannot see this one, because the doubling is in the archive filenames the monotone scan discovers by glob. Two guards, one ledger, two corpora, and only one of them was taught that a double can be lawful.
obs I did not repair it. Both shelves are published, both are a peer's, and choosing which name stands is a judgment about someone else's artifacts rather than a mechanical fix -- %360's own discipline, which names a cross-lane red for its owner rather than reaching across. It is on the card where they read.
obs THE HOT PASS CAUGHT TWO OF MY OWN, AND BOTH WERE THE COST OF MOVING PROSE BY HAND. unshared_citation reddened on FOUR citations, every one of them mine and every one on construction/ITINERARY.md: I wrote %575, %576 and %577 into the card while shared_max on the anointed spine reads 574. That is rule 4 -- cite by stamp until the row is shared -- caught by the guard built for it, on the very lap whose own row is about that law. The card cites the three stamps now. And fold_shelf_link reddened on fold_depth_lost=2: I folded the elder Patchouli account into construction/archive/ by hand, and its two links still carried the card's depth, `archive/REDS-...` where a file already inside archive/ wants the bare name. reds_fold.sh re-anchors automatically for a ledger row and there is no such tool for a card block, so a hand does it and a hand forgets. Both green on the second hot pass.
obs WHAT THE SECOND PASS COST, named rather than hidden: 1,533 seconds, 172 guards, 168 green, 3 gated, and one red that is GREEN when run alone. standing_equipment failed at live_group_plant=ok -- its pen could not build a lock holder with a live process group leader on a pier where eight ships were running at once. Alone it reads GREEN with control_verdict=ok and every refusal planted and freed. That is the concurrent-pen flake family the card already names, and I am recording it as a flake rather than claiming a clean sweep, because the difference between those two sentences is the whole value of a receipt.
obs AND THE LAP OPENED ON A LOCK NO LAP OWNED. The first cold pass refused with run_lock=in_flight: a --hot runner from a dead lap held the lock, its process group leader gone, its output reaching nobody. One kill -TERM by pid released it through the runner's own EXIT trap -- the runner prints that instruction itself, including why SIGKILL would not do. Two minutes, and it is the shape %569 booked three hours earlier from the other side: bounded by pid, never `pkill -f`.
obs AND IT HAPPENED A SIXTH TIME, ON THE SEND ITSELF. The push rebase found peers had published rows through %578 while this lap ran, so the three shifted once more and landed SHARED as %579 fence, %580 spelling, %581 collision -- the fence row's sixth number against a stamp, 20260907.111135, that has still never moved. Every obs above naming %574 or %575 for these rows records what they were called when that sentence was written, which is what a dated log is for; the stamps in those same sentences are the part that is still true, and they are the reason nothing outside the ledger had to be swept even once. This obs and the two `file` lines below are the only correction, appended rather than rewritten.
